{"item_id":"podcast:https://physicalaipodcast.co/episodes/010","version_id":"85cb287a2441351d8480acecc45d2ae3d6c8d1e3d180eee941a34d7bcda762d1","decision":"defer","reason":"This is fresh robotics context with timestamped passages, and the reported benchmark failures could matter for scrutiny of general models acting in physical space. But the sampled material is an automatic podcast transcript and does not identify the primary evaluation, its conditions, or an independently checkable result; the \"63 of 84\" figure and model-specific outcomes therefore remain unverified source claims. Defer pending the underlying benchmark and confirmation against the audio or primary report.","piece_ids":[]}
{"item_id":"youtube:4dbaTrlaLJ8","version_id":"b92f49a0aeaa5497e59c4087239e6125670fea5992a0c80ba51d2162575d7ffa","decision":"defer","reason":"The fresh Nvidia walkthrough is relevant supporting context because Newton is presented as an open-source, OpenUSD-based physics engine for robot simulation at 00:00:40–00:00:44. It does not supply a checkable comparison, disclosed settings, released validation result, or physical-transfer evidence; discussion of running trials and transferring motions at 00:44:25–00:44:29 is an aspiration rather than a demonstrated capability. Defer until code, benchmark conditions, or sim-to-real results can establish what was actually checked.","piece_ids":[]}
{"item_id":"youtube:7i5XHQozfQI","version_id":"582b91bc01228116ecd2d1c461bae76ac4715fc7ed39fa319cf08c96874fdc2e","decision":"reject","reason":"This is a fresh, timestamped official release walkthrough, and its configuration propagation and persistent center-of-mass features are plainly relevant to conventional CAD workflows. However, it is an incremental vendor feature announcement rather than frontier AI, agentic 3D, or new evidence about a capability a technical leader could evaluate. The vendor demonstration supports only that these interface features are being announced, not broader workflow or design-quality claims.","piece_ids":[]}
{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","decision":"accept","reason":"This fresh, timestamped Nvidia item directly addresses the lead themes of CAD-to-simulation asset conversion and verification: it distinguishes visual checks at 00:00:20, physics setup at 00:00:28, and foundation checks plus a grasp test at 00:00:37. Its credibility is limited to a vendor-described workflow and simulation checks; the full-sequence test at 00:00:47–00:00:50 does not establish manufacturing correctness or real-robot transfer. Accept as a useful, skeptical startup-and-builder watch framework, not as proof that the AI conversion is reliable.","piece_ids":["01","02","03"]}
{"item_id":"youtube:K10ckPj4EX8","version_id":"ab869f28930dfa68418f6cf465b421105d8869520aee70fe690c2c2c29d372e6","decision":"reject","reason":"The timestamped tutorial shows ordinary Fusion modeling operations, including sketches, constraints, sweeps, and 3D-print support considerations. It is relevant to introductory CAD practice but provides no frontier-model, agentic workflow, or independently checkable new capability. Its giveaway and tutorial-series framing also make it a poor fit for the publication's novelty and engineering-leader focus.","piece_ids":[]}
{"item_id":"youtube:Lnex2Q9bpvQ","version_id":"2d7286605286a5ff73bbb89d48b506a026e1112723a7ff7534475c34e4e5f48e","decision":"reject","reason":"This fresh, timestamped short explains existing Onshape custom-feature extensibility and gives workflow examples such as threads and laser-cut preparation at 00:00:39–00:00:44. It is conventional CAD instruction, not a newly evidenced frontier AI or intelligent-physical-systems development. The broad claim that custom features can speed up almost any workflow is vendor guidance without measured conditions.","piece_ids":[]}
{"item_id":"youtube:O8rLfw06qOo","version_id":"3e84409215650079dca0ffdb95ebde4d86c1ad4145552f7e1dcb3f95c7855d9f","decision":"reject","reason":"The customer story is fresh and relevant to CAD/CAM operations, including a reported reduction from three and a half hours to 45 minutes after moving to 3D toolpaths at 00:01:28–00:01:39. But it is a vendor-produced testimonial with one manufacturer’s self-reported result, no baseline conditions, attempt counts, or independent check, and it is not about frontier AI. It can illustrate conventional CAM adoption but does not meet the publication's novelty or credibility threshold.","piece_ids":[]}
{"item_id":"youtube:jLndwlduyzc","version_id":"a3209c32c4153b9ba8b68c0b0d0fc493c4e67807292a91e91e6e5a2c9c2cd938","decision":"reject","reason":"This fresh tutorial offers timestamped, concrete advice on robust parametric CAD, including query variables that follow faces rather than individually selected edges at 00:00:45–00:00:52. That is useful practitioner knowledge, but it is neither frontier AI nor a new agentic 3D capability, and the claimed resilience is demonstrated only by the publisher's example. The accepted set should seek a stronger practitioner perspective tied to AI-assisted geometry or verification instead.","piece_ids":[]}
{"item_id":"youtube:ys3z1c4LtyE","version_id":"666fe7b3f557e5944a3357051e9b14691050870ff2f273233d4f9f9885e3979e","decision":"reject","reason":"This is a fresh, timestamped manufacturer profile with CAD and fabrication context, but its central subject is a camper product and conventional design/manufacturing process. The claimed 6,000–7,000-pound advantage at 00:00:00 is an unverified promotional comparison, and no frontier AI, agentic CAD, or validation method is presented. It therefore misses the publication's relevance and evidence standards.","piece_ids":[]}
